 #:seon.task{:name "relaunched-is-read-only-for-leaves-with-an-ended-attempt"
             :plan "`seon.task/relaunched` is called by `seon.cluster.agent/launch-facts` for every ready leaf and costs 0.36 ms and 0.35 MB per leaf: 301 ms and 291 MB over 834 leaves (`docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/reactive-scheduling.md#2-measurements-default-warm-read-only`), though only a leaf with an ended attempt can be relaunched. What the work must be proportional to: the leaves with an ended attempt, found from one attempts-made query already read by `launch-facts`. Narrow the input of `seon.task/relaunched` (or derive it from the ended-attempt facts `launch-facts` holds) so a leaf with no ended attempt costs no call. Proof: to write before assignment (`seon.profile/measure` on `launch-facts` with 834 ready leaves and no ended attempt, `relaunched` zero calls)."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-closed-attempt-without-an-end-blocks-its-task"
             :plan "Live chain rough run 1 (`docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/live-chain-rough-1.md`, 2026-10-14): root closed a parked attempt (no end) with `my.root/close!`, which wrote `:seon.task/outcome :closed` and a reason but no `:seon.task.attempt/end`. `seon.cluster.agent/launch-facts` counts every attempt without an end as open, so setting the task `:running` again started nothing; root had to retract the attempt row by hand before attempt 2 launched. Done when closing an open attempt with `seon.task/close!` ends it, so a later `:running` on its task starts the next attempt (TASK-CHAIN's `my.root/run!` reads the same facts). Read `seon.task/close!`, `my.root/close!` and `seon.cluster.agent/launch-facts`."}]
